   "source": [
    "## Why can't we use Linear Regression instead logistic?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cf811f77-8eac-49c0-b447-3ddf51cd2a90",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This is the articulation style \n",
    "\n",
    "Notice the structure:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Business problem\n",
    "* What Linear Regression predicts\n",
    "* Why that prediction is a problem\n",
    "* How Logistic Regression solves it"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "e4fe866d-0e11-4a1e-a714-ce5474d494e7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Suppose I want to predict whether a student will pass or fail. The possible outputs are only Pass or Fail. If I use Linear Regression, it may predict values like 1.7 or -0.4. These values don't represent either Pass or Fail, so they are not meaningful. That's why Linear Regression is not suitable for classification problems. Instead, we use Logistic Regression because it predicts a probability between 0 and 1, and then converts that probability into a category like Pass or Fail."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "55aa916c-121c-4c2e-bb63-a33e5a5c7ea9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Suppose a bank wants to predict whether a customer will repay a loan. The possible answers are only Yes or No. If Linear Regression predicts 1.8, we don't know how to interpret that value because 1.8 is neither Yes nor No. Logistic Regression solves this problem by predicting a probability, such as 0.85, which means there is an 85% chance the customer will repay the loan. Since the probability is greater than 0.5, the final prediction is Yes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "c8f03a7f-e1e1-464f-943b-a0455c0fa6a0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Suppose Gmail wants to classify an email as Spam or Not Spam. If Linear Regression predicts 2.5, that output has no meaning because an email cannot be 2.5 times spam. Logistic Regression predicts a probability, for example 0.95, which means there is a 95% chance that the email is spam. Therefore, Gmail classifies it as Spam."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "998b97c3-e5e4-4bd4-944f-0db95c116621",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Suppose a company wants to predict whether a customer will buy a product. The possible outputs are Yes or No. If we use Linear Regression, it may predict values like 1.5 or -0.2, which cannot be interpreted as Yes or No. Logistic Regression predicts a probability between 0 and 1, such as 0.82. Since the probability is greater than 0.5, the model predicts that the customer will buy the product."
   ]
  },
  {
